      <h1>1099 vs. W-2 Tax & Pay: How Much More Should an Independent Contractor Make?</h1>
      <div class="article-meta"><span>Sep 14, 2026</span><span>·</span><span>7 min read</span></div>
      <p class="article-intro">When switching from a W-2 salaried job to a 1099 independent contractor role, an offer of $100,000 is not equal to a $100,000 salary. Here is how self-employment taxes, health benefits, and deductions impact your real take-home pay.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="container" style="max-width:740px;">
    <div class="prose">
      <div class="callout"><strong>The Contractor Rule of Thumb:</strong> As a 1099 independent contractor, you typically need to charge <strong>25% to 40% more</strong> than an equivalent W-2 hourly wage to break even after covering self-employment taxes, unpaid time off, and private health insurance.</div>

      <h2>The Fundamental Difference: W-2 vs. 1099</h2>
      <p>The distinction between a W-2 employee and a 1099 independent contractor comes down to tax withholding, employment laws, and control over how work is performed:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>W-2 Employee:</strong> Your employer withholds federal, state, and payroll taxes (FICA) directly from every paycheck. They also pay half of your Social Security and Medicare taxes, provide workers' compensation, and often offer paid benefits like health insurance, 401(k) matching, and PTO.</li>
        <li><strong>1099 Contractor:</strong> You are treated as a self-employed business owner. Clients pay you gross fees with zero tax withheld. You are responsible for paying the entire 15.3% self-employment tax, purchasing your own benefits, and filing quarterly estimated taxes with the IRS.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Hidden Cost: The 15.3% Self-Employment Tax</h2>
      <p>Under the Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA), every worker in the US pays into Social Security (6.2%) and Medicare (1.45%), totaling 7.65%.</p>
      <p>For a W-2 worker, the employer matches this 7.65%, making the total contribution 15.3%. However, when you work on a 1099 basis, the IRS considers you both the employee and the employer. You must pay both halves yourself through the <strong>Self-Employment (SE) Tax of 15.3%</strong> (12.4% Social Security up to the annual wage cap of $176,100, plus 2.9% Medicare with no cap).</p>
      <p>The silver lining: you can deduct the employer-equivalent portion (50% of your SE tax) from your gross income when calculating your adjusted gross income (AGI) on Form 1040.</p>

      <h2>Real Example: $100,000 W-2 vs. $100,000 1099 Take-Home Pay</h2>
      <p>Here is what happens to a $100,000 annual gross income under both structures for a single filer with no state income tax:</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Deduction / Expense</th><th>W-2 Employee ($100k)</th><th>1099 Contractor ($100k)</th><th>1099 Contractor ($130k)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Gross Income</td><td>$100,000</td><td>$100,000</td><td>$130,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FICA / Self-Employment Tax</td><td>-$7,650</td><td>-$14,130</td><td>-$18,369</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Federal Income Tax (approx)</td><td>-$12,800</td><td>-$11,200</td><td>-$16,900</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Health Insurance & Benefits</td><td>Covered by employer</td><td>-$6,000 (self-paid)</td><td>-$6,000 (self-paid)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paid Time Off (3 weeks unpaid)</td><td>Paid by employer</td><td>-$5,770 (unpaid time)</td><td>-$7,500 (unpaid time)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Net Estimated Take-Home</strong></td><td><strong>$79,550</strong></td><td><strong>$62,900</strong></td><td><strong>$81,231</strong></td></tr>
      </table>
      <p>As the table demonstrates, a 1099 contractor earning $100,000 takes home roughly <strong>$16,650 less</strong> per year than a W-2 employee with the same gross revenue. To achieve parity, the contractor must bill approximately $130,000/year.</p>

      <h2>Tax Advantages of Being a 1099 Contractor</h2>
      <p>While self-employment taxes are higher, 1099 contractors can take advantage of business write-offs that W-2 employees cannot:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Business Expense Deductions:</strong> Laptops, software subscriptions, cell phone bills, travel, and home office space directly reduce your net business profit before taxes are computed.</li>
        <li><strong>Qualified Business Income (QBI) Deduction:</strong> Eligible sole proprietors and LLC owners can deduct up to 20% of qualified business income under Section 199A, subject to income thresholds.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-Employed Health Insurance Deduction:</strong> You can deduct 100% of health, dental, and qualified long-term care insurance premiums directly on Schedule 1.</li>
        <li><strong>Solo 401(k) or SEP-IRA:</strong> High-earning contractors can shelter up to $70,000+ per year in retirement plans, far exceeding the standard $23,500 employee limit.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to Convert W-2 Salary to a 1099 Hourly Rate</h2>
      <p>To determine what hourly rate you must charge clients as a contractor, follow this 3-step formula:</p>
      <pre><code>Target W-2 Equivalent Salary = $90,000
1. Add 30% for taxes & overhead:  $90,000 × 1.30 = $117,000
2. Account for billable hours (assume 48 weeks × 35 billable hrs = 1,680 hrs):
3. Hourly Rate = $117,000 ÷ 1,680 hrs = $69.64/hour (round to $70/hr)</code></pre>

